WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.934
Safe implementation begins away from your primary system.

2
00:00:03.934 --> 00:00:10.778
Use a virtual machine, spare disk, lab laptop, external drive, or other recoverable test environment.

3
00:00:10.778 --> 00:00:17.387
RAM-backed boot designs often touch early boot, root filesystem layout, mounts, and persistence.

4
00:00:17.387 --> 00:00:26.434
A small mistake can leave a system unable to boot or can make changes disappear unexpectedly. Testing in a lab keeps learning reversible.

5
00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:31.312
Before changing a real system, back up important data and configuration.

6
00:00:31.312 --> 00:00:44.372
That includes bootloader configuration, initramfs configuration, fstab, crypttab, recovery keys, important files, and any distribution-specific configuration that affects root mounting.

7
00:00:44.372 --> 00:00:50.666
Keep recovery media available. Keep a known-good boot entry so you can return to a working path.

8
00:00:50.666 --> 00:01:01.759
If disk encryption, secure boot, vendor firmware, hybrid graphics, Wi-Fi firmware, or special storage drivers are involved, treat them as compatibility variables.

9
00:01:01.759 --> 00:01:10.255
Troubleshooting should be methodical. Check the kernel command line that actually booted. Verify which initramfs was loaded.

10
00:01:10.255 --> 00:01:17.493
Read boot logs and journal output when available. Confirm the root filesystem source. Inspect mount points.

11
00:01:17.493 --> 00:01:24.416
Check which layer is writable and where the upper layer lives. Confirm whether tmpfs size limits are set.

12
00:01:24.416 --> 00:01:31.576
Compare expected persistence with what actually survived reboot. Common failures come from assumptions.

13
00:01:31.576 --> 00:01:40.859
A driver needed for storage may be missing from initramfs. A root path may be wrong. A boot parameter may not be supported by the distribution.

14
00:01:40.859 --> 00:01:48.412
The system may not have enough RAM for the root image plus workload. A persistent mount may not appear early enough.

15
00:01:48.412 --> 00:01:54.234
A laptop may behave differently from a virtual machine because of firmware or graphics.

16
00:01:54.234 --> 00:02:03.753
Do not troubleshoot by randomly editing boot files on a primary machine. Change one thing at a time, keep notes, and preserve a working fallback.

17
00:02:03.753 --> 00:02:07.293
A safe RAM-boot project is not just a clever boot line.

18
00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:13.745
It is a tested design with recovery media, backups, known-good boot entries, and documentation.
